# Research Proposal for PhD Studies

## Adaptive Edge AI: Reversibility-Enabled Drift-Triggered Selective Model Updates on Microcontroller-Class Devices

---

## 1. Problem Statement

Edge AI systems are deployed on resource-constrained devices — microcontrollers with 264 KB of RAM, limited battery budgets, and intermittent connectivity. These systems run machine learning models that make predictions on sensor data in real time. In laboratory conditions, they perform well. In the field, they degrade silently over time.

This degradation occurs because edge environments are non-stationary. Sensors age, producing biased readings. Lighting and weather conditions shift. User behavior evolves. These changes cause a phenomenon called *concept drift*: the statistical relationship between input data and target labels changes, so the deployed model's predictions become less reliable [6]. A fixed model, frozen at deployment time, cannot adapt to these shifts. It either violates its accuracy requirements silently, or it violates its energy and latency budgets by attempting expensive retraining. Empirical evidence from production AI systems confirms the scale of the problem: a study of 128 model/data pairs across healthcare, transport, finance, and weather found temporal quality degradation in 91% of them [49]. Practitioner surveys report that fewer than half of production models are monitored at all, and that model performance issues such as drift are the number one runtime problem [43, 49].

Recent work has established that Edge AI in realistic deployments is necessarily adaptive [18]. Pittorino and Roveri introduce an Agent-System-Environment (ASE) lens that formalizes what changes, what is observable, and what can be reconfigured over time. Among the ten research challenges they identify, two are directly relevant to this proposal:

**C3 — Fault/Anomaly-Driven Targeted Updates.** When utility drops, can the system infer *why* it degraded (sensor fault, distribution shift, hardware issue) and apply the *smallest effective intervention* that restores performance? Current approaches trigger blanket retraining or full parameter updates, which is wasteful and often infeasible on constrained devices.

**C7 — Lifecycle Efficiency.** Over a long deployment horizon, the cost of *maintaining* a model (monitoring for drift, retraining, updating) can exceed the cost of running inference. The field has optimized inference efficiency extensively through quantization, pruning, and hardware acceleration. But the total cost of ownership for adaptive edge systems — the sum of execution and reconfiguration costs over time — remains understudied. Recent measurements confirm this asymmetry: on a RISC-V microcontroller, a single training update costs roughly five times the energy of one inference [44], and evaluation of edge AI lifecycles shows adaptation energy can exceed inference energy by an order of magnitude [46].

The core problem this PhD addresses is: **How can an edge device detect concept drift, attribute it to a specific cause, and apply a minimal, targeted, and reversible model update — all within a memory budget of a few hundred kilobytes and an energy budget of millijoules per update?**

---

## 2. State of the Art and Identified Gaps

### 2.1 Concept Drift Detection on the Edge

Concept drift detection is well established for server-class systems. DDM [6], ADWIN (Adaptive Windowing) [3], and Page-Hinkley test [20] are standard algorithms. On the edge, two landmark works demonstrate feasibility:

- **Disabato and Roveri (2022)** proposed TML-CD, the first concept drift adaptation system for Tiny Machine Learning on ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers [5]. It uses a deep feature extractor with a k-NN classifier and a hybrid passive-active adaptation module. This proved that drift handling *can* fit on MCUs, but the adaptation mechanism resets the full knowledge base — it does not perform targeted, minimal updates.

- **Yamada and Matsutani (2023)** proposed a fully sequential drift detection method cooperating with on-device sequential learning (OS-ELM) [22]. On a Raspberry Pi Pico (264 KB RAM), they achieved 88.9–96.4% memory reduction over batch-based methods with only 3.8–4.3% accuracy loss. Again, adaptation is global — the entire model is updated when drift is detected.

The most comprehensive survey of on-device learning in TinyML (Pavan et al., 2026) organizes roughly 70 works by their distribution change regime [25]. Two of its findings are directly relevant. First, the **concept drift regime is the thinnest slice of the on-device learning literature**, with fewer solutions than either the single-change or continual learning regimes. Second, **sensor calibration is the only real-world application addressed in the drift regime** — IMU calibration under thermal stress and pressure sensor calibration appear as isolated examples [25]. Everything else is evaluated on synthetic corruptions of standard benchmarks.

### 2.2 On-Device Learning on Microcontroller-Class Hardware

A substantial ecosystem for on-device learning on MCUs has emerged, but every approach works around the same bottleneck: the memory cost of backpropagation on devices with a few hundred kilobytes of SRAM.

- **Tiny Train Engine** (Lin et al., 2022) enables on-device training under 256 KB using quantization-aware scaling, sparse updates, and compile-time backward-graph pruning [48].
- **FQT on Cortex-M** (Deutel et al., 2024) trains fully quantized networks with dynamic partial gradient updates [38].
- **TinyFoA** (Huang and Aminifar, AAAI 2025) uses forward-only learning; **Poor Man's Training** (2024) uses zeroth-order methods; **TinyPropv2** (2024) uses dynamic sparse backprop.
- **COOL** (Liu et al., 2026) achieves continual online on-device learning on an STM32H743 using Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks, with ~46 ms per training step at ~2.8 mJ [31].
- **S³OvA** (Maoui and Benaboud, 2026) demonstrates incremental SVM learning on an ESP32-S [32].
- **Latent Replay Detection** (2026) is the first continual *detection* framework on MCUs at a 64 KB budget, but its authors state explicitly that fully on-device learning remains open *"due to memory constraints during backpropagation"* [39].

Two patterns matter here. Lightweight incremental learners (k-NN, SVM, OS-ELM) dominate the concept drift regime because they need no backprop [5, 22, 32], but they hit an expressiveness ceiling on complex sensor input, as the survey of Lourenço et al. documents [26]. Deep models adapt better but their training memory does not fit. Reversibility is a third path that the MCU community has not taken: because reversible layers reconstruct activations from the output, **no activation storage is needed for backpropagation at all**. This is the core insight of this proposal.

### 2.3 Selective and Targeted Model Updates

The idea of updating only part of a model is active in several communities, but has not reached MCU-class drift adaptation:

- **SEKF** (Hammond et al., 2025) updates only the parameters most affected by drift using a subset extended Kalman filter, and outperforms retraining on industrial process models [28]. But it is server-side, runs continuously (no drift detector), and uses no architectural support for selectivity.
- **Minerva** (Patil et al., EuroSys 2026) delivers component-granular model updates to deeply embedded microcontrollers with in-place swap, acceptance testing, and full-model rollback [30]. It has no drift detector and no selective parameter updates.
- **SoLA** (2026) introduces *reversible* model editing for LLMs: each edit is an independent LoRA module that can be revoked by deleting its routing key [29]. This is the closest existing work to the rollback mechanism this proposal needs, but it targets large language models, not embedded devices.
- Sparse and partial gradient updates exist on MCUs [38], and layer-freezing strategies exist for fleets [40, 47], but selectivity is treated as a cost-saving heuristic, not as a property of the architecture.

### 2.4 Reversible Neural Networks

Reversible neural networks (RevNets, i-RevNets) [8, 9] are architectures where each layer's activations can be reconstructed from the next layer's output. Originally developed for memory-efficient training, reversibility is now a major research direction:

- **RevViT** (Mangalam et al., 2022) achieves a 15.5x activation-memory reduction for vision transformers [36].
- **Dr²Net** (2024) is the first reversible family that can directly fine-tune pretrained non-reversible models, saving 46–80% memory [35].
- **BDIA** (Zhang et al., ICML 2025) achieves *exact bit-level reversibility while keeping the inference architecture unchanged*, treating transformer blocks as Euler steps of an ODE [33]. This directly answers the long-standing objection that reversibility constrains model design.
- **Reversible LLMs** (Gal et al., 2025) show ~10x activation-memory reduction and can retrofit already-trained models in about two epochs [34].

**Stefan Kuhn has published on reversible neural network architectures** [11, 13], including conditional invertible neural networks (cINNs) for bidirectional inference. One obstacle must be acknowledged and is addressed by this work: DaCapo (Khan et al., 2023) explicitly rejected RevNet-style reversibility for MCU on-device training, arguing that the same-input/output-dimension constraint limits model design [37]. This objection was made before BDIA and Dr²Net demonstrated that exact reversibility can be achieved *without changing the inference architecture* [33, 35]. This proposal directly builds on those results.

To our knowledge, **no reversible network has ever been trained on a microcontroller**, and no work applies reversible architectures to concept drift adaptation.

### 2.5 Distributed Adaptation

- **FedConD** [15] and **FL-HVD** [21] address concept drift in federated learning settings, but they assume coordinated training across nodes with a central aggregator.
- **FedDrift** (Jothimurugesan et al., AISTATS 2023) handles drift that is staggered in time and space, but again under a central server [41].
- **DriftGuard** (2026) performs selective group-level retraining under asynchronous drift with a retraining-cost objective — the closest existing system — but it is server-driven [40].
- A 2026 decentralized partial-model-fusion approach handles concept drift in a peer-to-peer setting, but does not coordinate fleet-level update decisions [43].

Systematic surveys of federated learning under concept drift identify *drift-aware aggregation* — who to aggregate, how to weight, when to split — as the central open algorithmic problem, together with the lack of standardized benchmarks [43, 46]. No existing work addresses the scenario where individual edge nodes experience *different* drifts at *different* times and must coordinate *minimal, targeted* updates without a central orchestrator.

### 2.6 Lifecycle Evaluation and TinyMLOps

Pittorino and Roveri's C7 challenge — lifecycle efficiency — calls for separating execution cost (C_run) from reconfiguration cost (C_chg) and introduces metrics such as Energy-to-Recover (E2R), Time-to-Recover (T2R), and Stability Score (SS) [18]. The Pavan et al. survey argues that *"without benchmarks that fix an explicit power budget and latency constraint, the co-optimization of execution and change cost becomes practically infeasible"* [25]. Energy accounting studies are emerging: EDGE-EVAL shows adaptation energy can be 6.4–7.2x inference energy [46]; ETuner shows lazy, selective updates save 64% time and 56% energy [47]; Cerberus measures per-update energy on RISC-V [44]. TinyMLOps for embedded devices is immature: a 2026 field study concludes that explicit monitoring and maintenance — drift detection, automated retraining triggers, hardware-degradation guards — is explicitly future work [45]. The evaluation protocol proposed in this PhD directly addresses this gap.

### 2.7 Identified Gap

The literature shows an opportunity at the intersection of five areas that have not been combined:

| Area | Existing Work | Gap |
|------|--------------|-----|
| Drift detection on MCUs | Disabato & Roveri (2022) [5], Yamada & Matsutani (2023) [22], S³OvA (2026) [32] | Full-reset or whole-model adaptation, not targeted |
| Selective updates | SEKF (2025) [28] (server), Minerva (2026) [30] (no drift trigger), SoLA (2026) [29] (LLMs) | None on MCU-class devices with drift attribution |
| Reversible NNs | Kuhn et al. [11, 13], RevViT [36], Dr²Net [35], BDIA [33], R-LLMs [34] | GPU/server only; never applied to drift adaptation; never trained on MCUs |
| MCU on-device learning | Tiny Train Engine [48], FQT [38], COOL [31], LRD [39] | Backprop memory unsolved; no reversibility |
| Fleet coordination of selective updates | DriftGuard (2026) [40] (central), FedDrift (2023) [41] (central) | Serverless, drift-driven, component-level: no existing work |
| Lifecycle evaluation | E2R/T2R/SS proposed [18] | No standardized benchmarks with explicit power/latency budgets [25, 27] |

This proposal fills this gap by combining lightweight concept drift detection with attribution on MCUs, **reversibility-enabled selective on-device learning** (activation-free backprop, drift-attributed block-level updates, built-in rollback), serverless fleet coordination under heterogeneous drift, and a lifecycle-efficiency evaluation protocol with explicit budgets.

---

## 3. Research Objectives and Questions

### Objective 1: Lightweight Drift Detection with Fault Attribution

**Research Question:** Can we design a drift detection and attribution method that identifies not only *that* drift occurred, but *which sensor, feature, or module* caused it, while operating within MCU memory budgets and O(1) per-sample computation?

Current drift detectors (DDM, ADWIN) output a binary signal: drift or no drift. They do not localize the cause. The C3 challenge requires attribution: is the utility drop caused by sensor drift, a distribution shift in a specific feature, or a hardware fault? We propose to extend lightweight distribution-based detection methods [22] with per-feature drift scores and change localization, inspired by multivariate fault attribution techniques [12, 23]. The flagship scenario is **sensor calibration and predictive maintenance on industrial IoT telemetry** — the only real-world application class found in the drift regime of the ODL literature [25], and one that matches the candidate's production anomaly-detection background.

**Methodology:**
- Implement and benchmark ADWIN2, DDM, Page-Hinkley, and KSWIN using the River library [19], TensorFlow Lite Micro [1], and the MOA framework [4] for reproducibility
- Extend these methods with per-feature drift attribution using an incremental feature-wise change test
- Evaluate on real sensor datasets with labeled fault sources: environmental drift, sensor aging, and hardware faults (TON_IoT, UCI gas sensor drift, IMU calibration)
- Metrics: detection delay, attribution accuracy (precision/recall per fault source), memory footprint, energy per detection

### Objective 2: Reversibility-Enabled Selective On-Device Learning

**Research Question:** Can reversible neural network architectures enable drift-triggered, selective model updates on microcontroller-class hardware, with an order of magnitude less energy and memory than full retraining, plus built-in rollback?

This is the heart of the proposal. The key insight is that invertible architectures (RevNets, i-RevNets, cINNs) maintain a bijective mapping between layers, which gives three properties useful on a microcontroller:

1. **Activation-free backpropagation.** Because activations are reconstructible, no activation memory is needed for training. This directly attacks the bottleneck that LRD explicitly identifies [39] and that the entire MCU training ecosystem works around with quantization, sparsity, and checkpointing [48, 38].
2. **Selective block updates.** If a specific module or feature space has drifted, the corresponding invertible block can be updated independently, without propagating changes through the full network.
3. **Built-in rollback.** A reversible model can return to its pre-drift state exactly, giving a recovery mechanism that today exists only as full-model swap (Minerva [30]) or LLM edit revocation (SoLA [29]).

We will address the DaCapo objection [37] head-on by building on BDIA-style exact reversibility with unchanged inference architecture [33] and Dr²Net-style retrofit of pretrained models [35], combined with conditional invertible designs from the supervisor's cINN work [11].

**Methodology:**
- Design a compact reversible architecture suitable for MCU-class devices (inspired by BDIA [33], i-RevNet [9], and Kuhn's cINN work [11])
- Implement selective block-level fine-tuning: when drift is attributed to feature group X, update only the invertible block processing X; evaluate rollback fidelity
- Compare against full model fine-tuning, incremental learning [24], sparse/partial-update baselines [38, 47], and knowledge-reset baselines [5, 22]
- Evaluate on TinyML benchmarks [2] and IIoT telemetry with controlled drift injection
- Metrics: accuracy recovery, energy per update (measured with a power probe, following the COOL protocol [31]), memory footprint for training, number of parameters modified, rollback fidelity

### Objective 3: Lifecycle-Efficient Fleet Coordination of Selective Updates

**Research Question:** In a multi-node edge deployment where different nodes experience different drifts at different times, what serverless coordination strategy minimizes total lifecycle cost (execution + reconfiguration) while maintaining system-level accuracy?

Edge deployments are distributed by nature. A fleet of 10–100 sensor nodes may experience heterogeneous drift patterns. Sharing update information across nodes could improve accuracy and reduce per-node computation, but it costs bandwidth and energy. Existing approaches are either centralized (DriftGuard [40], FedDrift [41]) or drift-blind (SwarmUpdate-class layer freezing [47]). The surveys identify drift-aware coordination as the central open problem [43, 46].

**Methodology:**
- Simulate a multi-node edge deployment (10–50 nodes) with heterogeneous, asynchronous drift injections
- Implement coordination strategies: independent adaptation, peer-to-peer selective-update sharing, and lightweight decentralized aggregation of selective updates (building on the reversible update mechanism from Objective 2: nodes share only affected invertible blocks)
- Compare against centralized baselines (DriftGuard-style) and federated baselines [16]
- Metrics: total lifecycle energy (Σ C_run + Σ C_chg per node), Energy-to-Recover [18], communication bytes per accuracy recovered, adaptation latency

### Objective 4: Lifecycle Evaluation Protocol with Explicit Power and Latency Budgets

**Research Question:** How should adaptive edge systems be evaluated so that long-horizon behavior, transients, and total cost of ownership are visible and comparable?

Every major recent survey calls for standardized evaluation of adaptive embedded ML [25, 27, 43, 46]. Current practice reports accuracy at a snapshot, which hides the energy asymmetry between inference and adaptation [46] and the cost of recovery [18].

**Methodology:**
- Implement the ASE evaluation protocol: time-series reporting of U(t), C_run(t), C_chg(t), V(t) [7, 18]
- Fix explicit power budgets and latency constraints per benchmark (the missing piece identified by Pavan et al. [25])
- Compute lifecycle metrics: Energy-to-Recover (E2R), Time-to-Recover (T2R), Stability Score (SS) [18]
- Publish benchmark streams with declared drift/intervention scripts and reference implementations
- Evaluate all three objectives under a unified protocol; compare against static deployment, full-retraining, and periodic-update baselines

---

## 4. Novel Contributions

This PhD is expected to make the following contributions:

1. **First drift detection method with integrated fault attribution** for MCU-class devices, evaluated on real industrial telemetry, enabling targeted (not blanket) adaptation
2. **First reversible-layer on-device learning system on microcontroller-class hardware** — activation-free backpropagation with drift-triggered selective block updates and built-in rollback; directly answers the DaCapo objection [37] with BDIA-style exact reversibility [33]
3. **First serverless coordination protocol for selective updates under heterogeneous edge drift**, minimizing lifecycle energy across a fleet
4. **First standardized lifecycle evaluation protocol with explicit power and latency budgets** (E2R/T2R/SS [18]) implemented on real embedded hardware
5. **Open-source benchmark suite** for lifecycle-efficient adaptive edge AI, including drift injection scripts, evaluation metrics, energy measurement procedures, and reference implementations

---

## 5. Research Plan and Timeline

### Year 1 (2027–2028): Foundations and Baseline

| Quarter | Activities | Deliverables |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Q1 | Literature deep dive: concept drift detection, online learning, TinyML, reversible NNs | Reading notes, annotated bibliography |
| Q2 | Implement drift detection benchmarks on laptop using River: ADWIN2, DDM, Page-Hinkley, KSWIN | Benchmarking code and results |
| Q3 | Port promising detectors to ESP32-S3 (TensorFlow Lite Micro); measure memory, latency, energy with a power probe | Ported detector, measurements |
| Q4 | Design and implement per-feature drift attribution; evaluate on TON_IoT, UCI sensor drift, IMU calibration | Attribution method prototype, paper draft |

**Milestone:** Conference paper on lightweight drift detection with fault attribution (target: IEEE ICMLA, ECML-PKDD, or ACM SenSys)

### Year 2 (2028–2029): Reversibility-Enabled Selective On-Device Learning

| Quarter | Activities | Deliverables |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Q1 | Study reversible NN architectures (BDIA, i-RevNet, cINN); design compact MCU-scale reversible blocks | Architecture design |
| Q2 | Implement activation-free backpropagation and selective block-level fine-tuning on ESP32-S3/STM32 | Implementation |
| Q3 | Benchmark selective vs. full updates under drift; measure energy per update with power probe; evaluate rollback | Benchmark results |
| Q4 | Integrate with drift attribution from Year 1; end-to-end C3 system on a single MCU | Integrated prototype, paper draft |

**Milestone:** Paper on reversible-NN-based targeted model updates for edge drift adaptation (target: IEEE TNNLS, NeurIPS, or DAC)

### Year 3 (2029–2030): Fleet Coordination

| Quarter | Activities | Deliverables |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Q1 | Simulate multi-node edge deployment; implement heterogeneous, asynchronous drift injection | Simulation framework |
| Q2 | Implement coordination strategies: independent, peer-to-peer selective sharing, decentralized aggregation | Coordination implementations |
| Q3 | Benchmark communication cost, energy, accuracy under different drift patterns; compare with centralized baselines | Benchmark results |
| Q4 | Design adaptive coordination strategy that selects update-sharing policy based on drift characteristics | Adaptive coordination policy, paper draft |

**Milestone:** Paper on distributed lifecycle-efficient adaptation (target: ACM/IEEE IoTDI, IEEE INFOCOM, or ACM MobiCom)

### Year 4 (2030–2031): Integration, Evaluation, and Thesis

| Quarter | Activities | Deliverables |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Q1 | Implement full lifecycle evaluation protocol with explicit power/latency budgets; compute E2R, T2R, SS | Evaluation suite |
| Q2 | Publish benchmark suite with drift injection scripts, energy measurement procedures, and reference implementations | Open-source benchmark |
| Q3 | Run long-horizon evaluations (30+ day deployments); finalize experiments | Experimental results |
| Q4 | Write and defend thesis | PhD thesis |

**Milestone:** PhD thesis defense; open-source benchmark suite publication

