## 8. Timeline and Feasibility

The PhD is proposed for four years (August 2027 – July 2031), aligned with the standard University of Tartu junior research fellow contract.

**Risk assessment:**

| Risk | Likelihood | Mitigation |
|------|-----------|------------|
| MCU memory constraints too tight for reversible architecture (the DaCapo objection [37]) | Medium | Build on BDIA-style exact reversibility with unchanged inference architecture [33]; start with a single reversible block; iterate toward full reversible networks if feasible |
| Lightweight learners outperform reversible nets on simple tasks | Medium | Target scenarios where deep adaptation matters (complex sensor input, multi-sensor fusion); publish the regime analysis as a contribution in itself |
| Drift attribution accuracy insufficient for selective updates | Medium | Evaluate multiple attribution methods; fall back to block-level updates (coarser granularity) |
| Fleet coordination costs exceed benefits | Low | Theoretical analysis before implementation; use simulation to find regimes where coordination helps |
| Energy measurement reproducibility | Low | Follow published measurement protocols (e.g., X-NUCLEO-LPM01A power shield as in COOL [31]) |

**Resources required:**
- ESP32-S3 / STM32F746 development boards (approximately $8–20 each; to be purchased in 2026)
- Power measurement equipment (STM32 Power Shield or Nordic Power Profiler-class)
- Sensor kits for real-world drift data collection (IMU, pressure, temperature)
- Standard compute hardware for simulation experiments (no GPU required for MCU-scale design; Colab-class resources suffice)
- Access to University of Tartu Distributed Systems Chair infrastructure

---

## 9. Expected Outcomes and Impact

### Scientific Impact
- First demonstration of reversibility-enabled, attribution-driven selective model updates for MCU-class devices, answering the DaCapo objection with modern reversible architecture results
- First application of reversible neural networks to concept drift adaptation
- First serverless coordination protocol for selective updates under heterogeneous edge drift
- A standardized evaluation paradigm for lifecycle-efficient adaptive edge AI

### Practical Impact
- Open-source benchmark suite with declared drift scripts, explicit power/latency budgets, and energy measurement procedures
- Reference implementations deployable on $8 microcontrollers
- Design principles for industrial edge deployments where reliability over time is critical (sensor calibration, predictive maintenance)

### Career Impact
- The PhD produces a researcher who can bridge ML systems and distributed systems — a skills profile in high demand for MLOps, edge AI, and AI infrastructure roles at companies deploying ML in production. The topic aligns with European funding priorities on decentralized AI and the cloud-edge continuum (Horizon Europe Cluster 4), and with the on-device learning platforms being industrialized by major vendors.

