# 12 — SOTA & Gap Analysis for the PhD Direction (Adaptive Edge AI)

> **Purpose:** Source of truth for the revised Stefan Kuhn research proposal and for Soham's
> confidence in the direction. Compiled from live literature research (sweeps A-D) and
> market/industry research, July 2026. Every load-bearing claim is cited; weak data is flagged.
> This document exists to answer: *what is the state of the art, where is the real gap,
> how bright is the future, and what is the realistic probability of a top-1% outcome.*

---

## 1. Research question being answered

Soham's PhD direction (with Stefan Kuhn, University of Tartu):

> **Adaptive Edge AI: drift-triggered, selective model updates on microcontroller-class
> hardware, enabled by reversible neural network architectures, evaluated with
> lifecycle-efficiency accounting, and coordinated across device fleets.**

The thesis spine is: **"reversibility as the enabler of selective on-device learning"** —
not merely "surgical repair via invertibility".

---

## 2. SOTA Sweep A — Concept drift + TinyML on-device learning (2023-2026)

Key works and what they mean for us:

| Work | Year | Finding relevant to us |
|---|---|---|
| Pavan, Pezzarossa, Pittorino, Roveri, Fafoutis — "What changes after deployment? A survey on On-device Learning in TinyML" (arXiv:2605.31226) | 2026 | Definitive ~70-work survey organized by *distribution change regime*. **The concept-drift regime is the thinnest slice of the literature.** Sensor calibration is the ONLY real-world application in the drift regime. Flags: no benchmarks fix explicit power/latency budgets, so "co-optimization of execution cost and change cost is practically infeasible"; persistent gap between methodological benchmarks and real deployments |
| Lourenço, Rodrigo, Gama, Marreiros — "On-device edge learning for IoT data streams: a survey" (arXiv:2502.17788) | 2025 | NN vs decision-tree split; explicit gap in memory optimization + tabular stream handling for on-device training; DTs hit expressiveness ceiling |
| Abozaid, Selim, Jaumard — "Adaptive Embedded Machine Learning on IoT Devices: A Review" (IEEE ITC-Egypt) | 2025 | Raises awareness of adaptability as underexplored; gaps: limited time-series adaptation, lack of standardized evaluation |
| Raghuwanshi — TinyML for wireless networks survey (arXiv:2606.30843) | 2026 | 146 studies; FL-based TinyML model updates for batteryless devices; update-aware checkpointing, fault-tolerant bootloader, intermittent-aware modify — all at survey/concept level only |
| Evolving ML survey (arXiv:2505.17902) | 2025 | Unified taxonomy; adaptive ensembles/meta-learning beat static under combined drift; **no standardized benchmarks for cross-domain validation** |
| FCL survey (arXiv:2606.11272) | 2026 | Open: heterogeneity under temporal drift, privacy-preserving memory, **lack of standardized benchmarks/evaluation protocols** |

**Net:** drift adaptation on MCU-class devices is real but thin; the evaluation problem is
called out by every major survey as unsolved; sensor calibration is the natural real-world
application.

---

## 3. SOTA Sweep B — Reversible/invertible neural networks (2023-2026)

| Work | Year | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| RevViT (Mangalam et al., CVPR) | 2022 | Reversible vision transformers; 15.5x activation-memory reduction; memory decoupled from depth |
| Dr²Net (arXiv:2401.04105) | 2024 | First reversible family that *directly fine-tunes pretrained non-reversible models* (dual residual, annealed coefficients); 46-80% memory savings |
| BDIA (Zhang, Lewis, Kleijn, ICML 2025) | 2025 | **Exact bit-level reversibility with an UNCHANGED inference architecture** (transformer blocks as ODE Euler steps). Defeats the classic objection that reversibility constrains architecture |
| R-LLMs (arXiv:2512.02056) | 2025 | Reversible LLMs from midpoint/leapfrog/Hamiltonian discretizations; ~10x activation-memory reduction; retrofit of existing pretrained models in ~2 epochs |
| VINA (arXiv:2602.20480) | 2026 | Variational invertible NN training with ERM-based guarantees |

Selective/targeted update work:

| Work | Year | Target | Gap vs us |
|---|---|---|---|
| SEKF — Subset EKF (Hammond et al., Scientific Reports) | 2025 | Servers/industrial process models | Selective parameter updates under drift, but continuous (no detector), non-reversible, no MCU |
| SoLA — reversible editing via semantic LoRA (arXiv:2603.11239) | 2026 | LLMs only | Edit-level rollback exists, but no drift, no edge, ICLR'26 withdrawn |
| Minerva (Patil et al., EuroSys) | 2026 | MCUs | Component-granular OTA + acceptance testing + FULL-MODEL rollback; no drift detector, pull-based, no reversibility |
| EMMET/ROME/MEMIT/LocFT-BF | 2024-2026 | LLMs | Knowledge editing; servers only |

**CRITICAL OBSTACLE FOUND:** DaCapo (Khan, Park, Seo, ACM TECS 2023) explicitly *rejected*
RevNet-style reversibility for MCU on-device training, claiming the same input/output size
constraint limits model design. **Counter-evidence exists**: BDIA (ICML 2025) achieves exact
reversibility *without changing the inference architecture*, and Dr²Net/R-LLM retrofit show
you can convert already-trained models cheaply. Beating the DaCapo objection with 2025-era
architecture results is a genuine, defensible contribution.

**Verdict: "reversible architectures + drift-triggered selective updates on MCU-class
hardware" = NOT FOUND anywhere in 2023-2026 literature. No reversible network has ever been
trained on a Cortex-M/ESP32/STM32.**

---

## 4. SOTA Sweep C — On-device learning on microcontrollers (2023-2026)

The MCU on-device-training ecosystem uses quantization, sparsity, checkpointing, zero-order
methods, or latent replay — **never reversibility**:

- Tiny Train Engine (Lin et al., NeurIPS 2022) — on-device training under 256 KB
- FQT Cortex-M (Deutel et al., 2024) — fully quantized training + partial gradient updates
- TinyFoA (AAAI 2025) — forward-only training
- TinyPropv2 (2024), Poor Man's ZO (2024), TinyTrain (ICML 2024)
- ASI (ICML 2025) — 120x activation-memory reduction
- **LRD (arXiv:2603.00138, Feb 2026)** — first continual *detection* on MCUs at 64 KB;
  explicitly states: *"Enabling fully on-device continual learning ... remains an open
  challenge due to memory constraints during backpropagation."*
- **COOL (CCF Trans. Pervasive Comp. Interact., 2026)** — KAN-based continual on-device
  learning on STM32H743; ~46 ms training, ~2.8 mJ/training cycle (with X-NUCLEO-LPM01A
  power measurements — a good protocol to copy)
- **S³OvA (Informatica, 2026)** — incremental SVM on ESP32-S, +20 accuracy points, memory stable

**Net:** the memory bottleneck for backprop is the acknowledged open problem; lightweight
learners (SVM/kNN/OS-ELM/KAN) dominate the drift regime but hit expressiveness ceilings on
complex sensor input [Sweep A: Lourenço].

---

## 5. SOTA Sweep D — Distributed adaptation + TinyMLOps (2023-2026)

**Verified: the fleet-level gap is OPEN.** No work lets edge nodes collectively decide which
model components to update on which nodes under heterogeneous drift, without a central
orchestrator, under a lifecycle-energy objective.

| Work | Year | Selective? | Drift-driven? | Decentralized? | Missing |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| DriftGuard (arXiv:2603.18872) | 2026 | Yes (MoE groups) | Yes (asynchronous) | No — server | Central orchestrator |
| NOLTA 2026 partial model fusion | 2026 | Yes (components) | Yes (eval) | Yes (P2P) | No coordinated fleet plan |
| SwarmUpdate (arXiv:2503.13784) | 2025 | Yes (layers) | No (static) | Hierarchical | Drift-awareness |
| FedDrift (AISTATS 2023) | 2023 | No | Yes (staggered) | No | Selectivity + decentralization |
| FedConD (2021/2023), FL-HVD (2023/2026), FedDriftGuard (2026) | — | No | Yes | No | All server-based |
| Minerva (EuroSys 2026) | 2026 | Component granularity | No trigger | Pull-based | Drift-aware targeting |
| Pittorino & Roveri position paper (arXiv:2604.07360) | 2026 | Calls for it (C8) | — | — | Position only; names E2R/T2R/SS metrics, C7 lifecycle efficiency |

FL + drift surveys (Electronics 14(22):4480, 2025; FCL arXiv:2606.11272, 2026) name as open
problems: **drift-aware aggregation** (who/weight/split), scalable drift detection on
resource-limited devices, missing convergence guarantees, lack of standardized benchmarks.

TinyMLOps is immature: TinyMLOps framework (River Publishers, 2025) exists, but the June 2026
field study (Scaramuzza & Tamburri, arXiv:2606.20952) concludes *"explicit monitoring and
maintenance — concept-drift detection, automated retraining triggers, hardware-degradation
guards"* is **explicitly future work**.

Energy accounting exists only in fragments: EDGE-EVAL (2026) found adaptation can cost
6.4-7.2x inference energy (invisible in accuracy benchmarks); ETuner (2024) lazy updates save
64% time / 56% energy; Cerberus (IEEE JSEN 2026) measured 29.65 mJ/update vs 5.49 mJ/inference
on RISC-V.

---

## 6. Market evidence — how bright is the future (comparative)

### 6.1 Market size and growth (multi-source; definitions differ — cite ranges)

| Source | Numbers |
|---|---|
| Technavio (May 2026) | TinyML $2.23B (2025), CAGR 35.7% (2026-2030), +$8.06B incremental |
| ABI Research (2024-25) | TinyML chipset shipments 37% CAGR through 2031, >4.1B units; revenue >$7.8B; NPUs 90% CAGR; Europe edge AI 17% CAGR |
| Nordic Semiconductor (2025) | TinyML chipset $5.9B by 2030 (industry citation) |
| MarketsandMarkets (2025) | Edge AI hardware $26.14B (2025) -> $58.90B (2030), 17.6% CAGR; edge AI software $2.4B -> $8.89B (2031), 24.4% CAGR |
| Grand View (2026) | Edge AI $24.91B (2025) -> $118.69B (2033), 21.7% CAGR |
| **MarketsandMarkets (key quote)** | Edge AI *training* function grows faster than inference, driven by "federated learning ... device-based model updates to accommodate real-time learning and adaptation to local conditions" — **analysts name Soham's PhD topic as the growth driver** |

### 6.2 Drift is the industry problem (the pain this PhD solves)

- **Nature Scientific Reports (Vela et al., 2022): temporal degradation in 91% of 128
  model/dataset pairs** (healthcare, transport, finance, weather)
- Gartner (2025): 60% of AI projects abandoned through 2026; 63% lack AI-ready data
- Practitioner surveys: <50% of production models monitored (n=188, 2024); model performance
  issues (drift, data quality) = #1 runtime problem at 38% (n=91, 2025); 84.3% say
  detection/diagnosis time is a problem (n=945)
- IBM: model accuracy "can degrade within days of deployment"

### 6.3 On-device learning is being industrialized (vendors moving into our exact topic)

Apple (on-device LoRA personalization, WWDC 2025), Google (federated on-device
personalization on Android from Q3 2025; Coral NPU Oct 2025), ST (STM32N6, 600 GOPS NPU),
Nordic (acquired Neuton.AI June 2025; nRF54LM20B NPU), Edge Impulse ($34M), Plumerai
($8.7M Sept 2025), Axelera AI (>€200M, EIC-backed).

### 6.4 Jobs: the scarce "bridge" profile

- LinkedIn (Apr 2026): AI-skills demand +142% YoY; Europe has **1.9 qualified candidates per
  10 open AI roles**; ML Engineer postings +178% YoY
- UK: <10,000 engineers list edge skills vs ~4,000-8,000 live edge vacancies (+39% YoY);
  real TinyML role advertised at £92k; **TinyML UK Network launched 2026** to fix skills gap
- Germany: senior ML systems / MLOps / edge hybrid €105-140k (recruiter data); premiums for
  production monitoring + ML depth combinations; BMW, NVIDIA Munich, Flexlith, Formula hiring
  edge-ML/embedded AI roles with DevOps+ML hybrid requirements (July 2026 postings)
- US: "TinyML Engineer" averages $145k (Orbyt 2026)

### 6.5 EU funding matches the topic 1:1

- **HORIZON-CL4-2027-04-DATA-03: €35M — "decentralised AI compute ... whole AI model
  lifecycle ... across the cloud-edge continuum" (opens Nov 2026 — Soham's application window)**
- HORIZON-CL4-2025-04-DATA-02: €30M cognitive computing continuum (closed Oct 2025, 74 proposals)
- EIC 2026: >€2.125B total

### 6.6 Comparative brightness vs alternative PhD directions (judgment, structured)

| Direction | Demand/supply | Funding | Differentiation for Soham | Top-1% ceiling |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Adaptive edge AI (this choice)** | Very scarce (1.9/10, <10k edge-skilled) | EU calls match exactly | HIGH: DevOps + verified gap + reversible NNs | High |
| LLM/NLP research | Crowded (2.3/10 global, still hot) | High but saturated | LOW: no NLP background, everyone does this | High but crowded; harder admission |
| General ML/DL | Oversupplied | Moderate | LOW-MEDIUM | Medium |
| MLOps/AI infrastructure | High | Moderate | HIGH fit, but less "PhD-shaped" (usually career via industry) | Medium-High |
| Robotics/autonomy | High | High | LOW today (no robotics credentials) -> low admission odds | High only via elite labs |
| Stay in DevOps, no PhD | Good now | n/a | n/a | Low-Medium (€40-90k trajectory, slower ceiling) |

**How bright: 8-9/10 relative to alternatives for HIS profile.** The combination of a
verified novel gap + analyst-named growth driver + matching EU funding + vendor
industrialization + scarce hybrid job profile is rare. Main honest caveats: TinyML market
sizing is definition-dependent (2B vs 7B by 2030) and "TinyML engineer" as a literal title
barely exists in Germany yet — demand materializes as hybrid MLOps/edge roles.

---

## 7. Top-1% probability assessment (honest estimate, judgment-based)

**Method:** base rates for PhD -> top-tier outcome, multiplied by Soham's differentiators,
discounted by execution risk. This is a structured judgment, not a measurement.

- Base rate for a generic CS/ML PhD holder landing a "top-1%" outcome (top-tier salary
  €120k+ / elite research role): **~15-25%** (competitive but many never differentiate).
- Soham's profile-specific baseline (no PhD, staying in ops): top-1% outcome ~5-10%.

**Multipliers from this direction, if executed well:**
1. Verified novel gap with 3-4 "firsts" (papers get visibility/citations): x1.5-2
2. Market timing (analysts name the topic; vendors shipping it): x1.5-2
3. Scarcity of the bridge profile (DevOps + TinyML + drift; 1.9 candidates/10 roles): x2-3
4. EU funding alignment (Horizon calls match thesis -> consortium/industry roles): x1.5
5. Execution risk (no pubs yet, self-taught ML, DaCapo technical risk, hardware to buy): x0.5-0.7

**Estimated probability of a top-tier (top-1% class) outcome after a well-executed PhD:
40-70%** (central estimate ~50%). With mediocre execution: 20-30%. Without the PhD: 5-10%.

**What moves the needle toward 70%:**
- Publish early: Objective 3 (fleet coordination) is simulation-friendly -> first paper in
  Year 1-2; publish the MCU demo (ESP32 drift detection + selective update) as open source
- Ship a visible artifact early (GitHub: River benchmarks -> ESP32 demo)
- Do an industry internship / collaboration with Nordic, ST, Edge Impulse-type companies
- Get into a Horizon consortium via Stefan's network (the €35M call opens Nov 2026)
- Network with the Swedish track (Priyanka/Palu/Emelie) as Plan B in parallel

---

## 8. Verified gap candidates (all re-checked July 2026)

| # | Gap | Verification | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| G1 | Reversibility-enabled drift-triggered selective updates on MCU-class hardware (activation-free backprop + block-level selective updates + built-in undo) | OPEN — not found in 2023-2026; must beat DaCapo (TECS 2023) objection; answer: BDIA/Dr²Net retrofit | Medium |
| G2 | Fleet-level coordination of selective updates under heterogeneous drift, serverless, lifecycle-energy objective | OPEN — DriftGuard is server-driven; NOLTA'26 uncoordinated | Medium-low (simulation) |
| G3 | Standardized lifecycle evaluation with explicit power/latency budgets (co-optimize C_run + C_chg), E2R/T2R/SS on real hardware | OPEN — every major survey demands it (Pavan 2026, Abozaid 2025, FCL 2026) | Low |
| G4 | Reversibility for intermittent/batteryless learning (reversible nets eliminate activation checkpoints) | OPEN — Raghuwanshi 2026 is survey-level only | High (hardware complexity) |
| G5 | Deep on-device adaptation for tabular/operational streams (IIoT telemetry) | OPEN but partial; less crisp as a thesis spine | Low-Medium |

**Recommended: G1 as the spine, G2 + G3 as the second and third pillars, G5 as the flagship
application (sensor calibration / predictive maintenance on TON_IoT), G4 as stretch goal.**

